1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:27,776
On the day, the book is down 540 dollars.

7
00:00:27,776 --> 00:00:31,957
iShares Asia 50 ETF (AIA) leads, +3.1 percent.

8
00:00:31,957 --> 00:00:34,320
Apple (AAPL) lags at -2.5.

9
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:39,227
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:39,227 --> 00:00:41,854
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:41,854 --> 00:00:45,518
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:45,518 --> 00:00:48,560
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:54,091
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:00:54,041 --> 00:01:01,879
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:01,879 --> 00:01:10,051
12 positions, led by APA Corporation, ticker APA, Chevron, ticker CVX, ConocoPhillips, ticker COP.

16
00:01:10,051 --> 00:01:21,391
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:21,347 --> 00:01:26,242
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:26,242 --> 00:01:28,690
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:39,442
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:39,442 --> 00:01:43,550
Inside the book today: 2 positions up, 10 down.

21
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:45,911
Best, AIA at +3.14 percent.

22
00:01:45,911 --> 00:01:47,659
Worst, APA at -3.19.

23
00:01:47,659 --> 00:01:52,729
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:01:52,729 --> 00:01:59,984
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -4.95% per trade over 340 graded positions.

25
00:01:59,984 --> 00:02:07,152
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

26
00:02:07,152 --> 00:02:12,397
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:12,397 --> 00:02:28,482
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 229 names close the day in Stage 2; 2 new invalidations queue exit reviews; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:28,452 --> 00:02:30,719
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:30,719 --> 00:02:40,070
Day 45 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:44,981
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 102,517.

31
00:02:44,981 --> 00:02:51,876
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 2.7 thousand back at 99,775.

32
00:02:51,876 --> 00:02:59,149
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 4.3 thousand back at 98,193.

33
00:02:59,149 --> 00:03:01,793
The race has no finish line.

34
00:03:01,793 --> 00:03:06,138
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:06,138 --> 00:03:11,050
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:12,939
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:12,939 --> 00:03:21,846
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:21,808 --> 00:03:29,143
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:29,143 --> 00:03:31,385
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:31,385 --> 00:03:34,373
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
